.TH clri "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
clri \- clear i-node
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/clri \fIfilesystem inumber ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
clri -- clear i-node
Usage:	/etc/clri filesystem inumber ...
.HE
.B clri
zeroes each i-node named by an
.I inumber
argument on
.IR filesystem .
.I filesystem
should be the raw (character-special) device holding the file
system, e.g.
.BR /dev/rhd0 .
.B clri
reads the superblock of
.I filesystem
to locate the i-node list, then, for each
.IR inumber ,
reads the disk block containing that i-node, zeroes the i-node's
entry in the in-memory copy of the block, and writes the block
back.
.PP
The i-node is identified purely by number; no check is made that it
is of any particular type or that it is otherwise a sensible thing
to clear.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR check ,
.B mkfs
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B clri
prints a diagnostic and exits 1 if
.I filesystem
cannot be opened, its superblock cannot be read, an
.I inumber
is out of range for the file system, or a read or write error
occurs.
.SH NOTES
.B clri
operates directly on the raw device and does not go through the
file system; run it only on an unmounted file system, since any
in-memory copy of the affected i-node will otherwise overwrite this
change when it is later written back to disk.
If the i-node cleared belongs to the root file system, reboot
immediately afterward.
